 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 17,
   "y": 36,
   "plaque": "cambridge_equiano",
   "title": "Olaudah Equiano",
   "text": "Olaudah Equiano was kidnapped as a child in what is now Nigeria and enslaved. He bought his freedom in 1766 and in 1789 published his life story, The Interesting Narrative, a bestseller and a weapon in the campaign against the slave trade. In 1792 he married Susannah Cullen at Soham, up the road in Cambridgeshire.",
   "people": "black_britain"
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 53,
   "text": "CAMBRIDGE. Bridge over the Cam, as the name says. North to the Fens and Ely, south to London. Cyclists have right of way. Cyclists believe they always have right of way."
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 12,
   "text": "THE APPLE TREE. Grown from Isaac Newton's own tree at Woolsthorpe in Lincolnshire, where he watched an apple fall and got thinking about why things fall at all. Newton was a student, then a professor, at Trinity.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_newton_tree",
   "title": "Newton's Apple Tree"
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "KING'S COLLEGE CHAPEL. Begun by Henry VI in 1446, finished under Henry VIII in 1515. Inside is the largest fan vault in the world, and some of the finest old stained glass in England.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_kings_chapel",
   "title": "King's College Chapel"
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 35,
   "text": "THE MATHEMATICAL BRIDGE. Built in 1749 by James Essex to a design by William Etheridge. Legend says Isaac Newton built it without a single nut or bolt, and when students took it apart they could not put it back. Newton died in 1727. The bolts were there all along.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_maths_bridge",
   "title": "The Mathematical Bridge"
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 34,
   "text": "THE CORPUS CLOCK. A gold clock with no hands, ridden by a monstrous grasshopper called the Chronophage, the time-eater. Unveiled by Stephen Hawking in 2008. It is only exactly right once every five minutes. On purpose.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_corpus_clock",
   "title": "The Corpus Clock"
  },
  {
   "x": 50,
   "y": 33,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: On 28 February 1953 Francis Crick and James Watson walked in here and announced they had found the secret of life: the double helix structure of DNA. Rosalind Franklin's X-ray photograph from King's College London had been the vital clue.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_eagle_dna",
   "title": "The Eagle and DNA",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": 0.475
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 13,
   "text": "CLARE BRIDGE. Built in 1640, the oldest of the college bridges still standing. Fourteen stone balls sit along its sides. One has a wedge cut out of it. Builders' joke, revenge, or a slip of the chisel? Nobody knows, and the ball isn't saying.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_clare_bridge",
   "title": "Clare Bridge"
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 43,
   "text": "PUNTING. Flat-bottomed boats you push along with a long pole off the riverbed. Cambridge style: stand on the flat end, the till. Oxford stands at the other end. Both are certain the other is wrong. Rule one: if the pole sticks, let go of the pole, not the punt.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_punting",
   "title": "Punting on the Cam"
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 3,
   "text": "THE BACKS. Where the colleges back on to the Cam, with lawns and willows down to the water. The best view of King's Chapel is from here, ideally from a punt, ideally not falling out of it.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_the_backs",
   "title": "The Backs"
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "MARKET HILL. There's been a market in the middle of Cambridge since Saxon times. Every day of the week you can buy fruit, books, bike locks and, inevitably, more bike locks.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_market_hill",
   "title": "Market Hill"
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 3,
   "text": "MIDSUMMER COMMON. Cows still graze here, in the middle of a city, as they have for hundreds of years. The Strawberry Fair and the Midsummer Fair both happen on it. The cows are unimpressed by both.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_midsummer_common",
   "title": "Midsummer Common"
  },
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 46,
   "text": "THE UNIVERSITY. Founded in 1209 by scholars who left Oxford after a row with the townsfolk. It's now 31 colleges, and has taught Newton, Darwin, Turing, Milton and more Nobel prizewinners than most countries.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_university",
   "title": "The University of Cambridge"
  },
  {
   "x": 60,
   "y": 24,
   "plaque": "eastanglia_arm",
   "title": "The ARM Chip",
   "text": "In 1985, at Acorn Computers in Cambridge, Sophie Wilson and Steve Furber's new processor, the Acorn RISC Machine, ran for the first time. It was simple and sipped power so gently that, the story goes, the first one worked before its power supply was even connected. That frugality took it into phones. ARM designs are now in nearly every mobile phone in the world."
  },
  {
   "x": 64,
   "y": 24,
   "plaque": "eastanglia_raspberry_pi",
   "title": "Raspberry Pi",
   "text": "The Raspberry Pi Foundation, a Cambridge charity, worried that fewer young people knew how a computer worked inside. Its answer was a bare circuit board the size of a credit card, launched on 29 February 2012 for 35 US dollars. Millions have been sold since, to classrooms, bedrooms, robots and garden weather stations."
  },
  {
   "x": 68,
   "y": 24,
   "plaque": "eastanglia_alphafold",
   "title": "AlphaFold",
   "text": "Proteins are the busy little machines in every living cell, and their shape decides what they do. Working out one shape could take a lab years. DeepMind's AlphaFold learnt from about 170,000 shapes scientists had already found, and by 2020 could predict new ones, often nearly as well as the lab. With the European Bioinformatics Institute at Hinxton, just south of here, it has shared predictions for over 200 million proteins, free. Demis Hassabis and John Jumper shared in the 2024 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for it."
  }
 ],
